.\"
.TH "CSSNUMERICVALUE.EQUALS" "3JS" "October 20, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSNumericValue.equals \- CSSNumericValue: equals() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]equals()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] interface returns \f[CR]true\f[R] if every
value passed to it is the same as the \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R], and
\f[CR]false\f[R] otherwise.
.PP
The method compares how the values are written, not what they compute
to.
Single values, such as those represented by \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R], are
the same when they have the same \f[CR]value\f[R] and \f[CR]unit\f[R].
Units aren\(cqt converted, so \f[CR]CSS.in(1)\f[R] isn\(cqt the same as
\f[CR]CSS.cm(2.54)\f[R], even though both are one inch long.
Calculations, such as sums represented by \f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R], are the
same when they are the same kind of calculation and contain the same
values in the same order.
So \f[CR]calc(1px + 2px)\f[R] isn\(cqt the same as
\f[CR]calc(2px + 1px)\f[R], or as \f[CR]3px\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
equals()
equals(number1)
equals(number1, number2)
equals(number1, number2, /* \&..., */ numberN)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]number1\f[R], \&..., \f[B]numberN\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Either a number or a \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R].
A number is compared as a value without a unit, like one created with
\f[CR]CSS.number()\f[R].
.SS Return value
\f[CR]true\f[R] if every value passed is the same as the
\f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R], and \f[CR]false\f[R] otherwise.
If no values are passed, the method returns \f[CR]true\f[R].
.SS Exceptions
None.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
Values with the same number and unit are the same, and so are sums of
the same values in the same order:
.IP
.EX
console.log(CSS.px(10).equals(CSS.px(10))); \f[I]// true\f[R]

\f[B]const\f[R] sum = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathSum(CSS.px(1), CSS.px(2));
console.log(sum.equals(\f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathSum(CSS.px(1), CSS.px(2)))); \f[I]// true\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Changing the order of the values in a sum, comparing a sum to its
result, or writing the same length in a different unit, makes the values
different:
.IP
.EX
console.log(sum.equals(\f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathSum(CSS.px(2), CSS.px(1)))); \f[I]// false\f[R]
console.log(sum.equals(CSS.px(3))); \f[I]// false\f[R]
console.log(CSS.in(1).equals(CSS.cm(2.54))); \f[I]// false\f[R]
.EE
.SS Comparing several values
When several values are passed, the method returns \f[CR]true\f[R] only
if each of them is the same as the \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R].
A number is compared as a value without a unit:
.IP
.EX
console.log(CSS.number(5).equals(5, CSS.number(5))); \f[I]// true\f[R]
console.log(CSS.px(10).equals(CSS.px(10), CSS.px(11))); \f[I]// false\f[R]
.EE
